       },
       {
        "text": "I've built the nf-core/sarek tumour\u2013normal run, but it can't be submitted until you confirm which sample is the tumour and which is the normal.\n\n**Tumour/normal pairing** (I read tumour or normal from each sample's name, since no other field records it):\n\n| patient | sample | name | status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NTC-TN-p1 | D.SEQ-261028BMC-5 | NTC-TN-p1-tumour-WGS | **1 (tumour)** |\n| NTC-TN-p1 | D.SEQ-261028BMC-6 | NTC-TN-p1-normal-WGS | **0 (normal)** |\n\n**Samples:** one samplesheet with 2 rows. Both are paired-end 2\u00d7150 whole-genome sequencing from genomic DNA, run on a NovaSeq 6000. I used the patient label NTC-TN-p1, taken from the sample names, and set the lane to L001.\n\n**Run settings:**\n- **Genome:** none of the metadata names the organism. The files come from nf-core's human (homo_sapiens) test data, so I set the genome to human GRCh38. It uses the local Luria reference (`GATK.GRCh38/Homo_sapiens_assembly38.fasta`).\n- **Aligner:** bwa-mem.\n- **Variant callers:** Mutect2 and Strelka for small somatic mutations, and Manta for structural variants. Whole-genome mode, so no capture-region BED file is needed.\n- Annotation tools (VEP, snpEff) and copy-number tools (ASCAT, Control-FREEC) can't be used here, because their reference files aren't set up on Luria.\n\n**Run time:** roughly 8\u201324.5 hours from when the job starts on Luria, plus however long it waits in the queue. That covers aligning and calling 2 whole genomes. It's a first estimate, not yet measured on real runs.\n\nWhen the run finishes, just tell Nessie \"my run is done\" and she'll turn the outputs into NExtSEEK upload workbooks for you to review.\n\nIs the tumour/normal table right? Reply to confirm, or tell me what to change, such as the callers, aligner or genome.",
        "type": "text"
       }
      ],
